cad-path-dotdot-resolution — open/findfile/load and ".." segments differ (BricsCAD vs clautolisp)
=================================================================================================

Reported from: porting the SCHMS+ test harness
(sncf-reseau/src/schms, tests/test-unitaire.lsp) to `alfe --bricscad
--mode batch`. alfe 1.1.16, BricsCAD V26, macOS.

Summary
-------

A path containing `..` segments (e.g.
`/…/schms/tests/../../outils-autolisp/autolisp-test/test-framework.lsp`)
is resolved by clautolisp's file primitives but NOT by BricsCAD's:

- clautolisp: `open`/`findfile`/`load` collapse `..` and reach the file.
- BricsCAD: the same path fails (file treated as not found).

Evidence
--------

The harness originally built such paths
(`(strcat *tu:repo-dir* "/../outils-autolisp/…")`). On clautolisp the
whole SCHMS+ suite runs (177 OK). On BricsCAD the framework `load`
fails. Rewriting the harness to emit CLEAN absolute paths (no `..`)
makes BricsCAD's `open` succeed:

    ;; BricsCAD, clean absolute path -> OK
    (open "/Users/.../src/outils-autolisp/autolisp-test/test-framework.lsp" "r")  => <file>

Repro to confirm (needs an EXISTING file reached via "..")
----------------------------------------------------------

    ;; with <F> = an existing file, <D> its directory:
    (open "<D>/x/../<basename F>" "r")
    ;; clautolisp: returns a file handle
    ;; BricsCAD:   expected nil  (please confirm)

(The first quick check used a non-existent target, so it was
inconclusive — hence "please confirm".)

Expected
--------

For parity, BricsCAD-backed `open`/`findfile`/`load` should resolve
`..` (and `.`) segments like clautolisp, OR this divergence should be
documented so callers normalize paths first.

Notes
-----

Worked around on the SCHMS+ side by constructing clean paths (no `..`),
so this is not blocking the harness — filing per the CAD/clautolisp
parity rule.


==> . and .. are standard POSIX directory entries. The semantic is clear
    (POSIX walks the physical directories).
    Note: in Common Lisp, there's the distinction between :up and :back
    https://www.lispworks.com/documentation/HyperSpec/Body/19_bbdc.htm

    if .. is considered as UP (this is the POSIX definition)
    then in /a/b/c/../d/e/f
         we go to /a/b/c/
         then we go up one level to a directory which
              depends on whether c was a symbolic link!
         then we go forward down d/e/f
              It could be /a/b/d/e/f if c was a normal directory,
              or eg. /x/y/d/e/f if c was a symbolic link to /x/y/q

   if .. is considered as BACK
   then it's a mere syntactic rewriting of the path:
                       /a/b/c/../d/e/f
     is rewritten as   /a/b/d/e/f
     there's no consideration on c, it could even not exist.

   clautolisp must implement the UP semantics of POSIX path.

   This should be documented, and the fact that bricscad produces
   different results must be documented too.

   And a test for autocad must be scheduled.

   When paths containing /../ are encountered in non --clautolisp or
   --lax dialects, must be flagged (a warning issued).

===========================================================================
PROBE RESULTS (2026-07-30) — the divergence is OS-level, and the
original hypothesis is refuted. clautolisp is UP on every platform.
===========================================================================

Confirmed with a self-contained probe on the real runners (pjb granted
the macOS + Windows CAD runners for this): pathname-probe.lsp
(autolisp-front-end/tests/scenarios/entities/) builds BASE/ and BASE/sub/,
writes BASE/target.txt, then opens it three ways. The CI jobs are
pathname:probe:* (now `when: manual`); drivers run-pathname-probe.{sh,ps1}.

Two cases matter:
  - EXISTING intermediate:  open "BASE/sub/../target.txt"     (sub/ exists)
  - MISSING  intermediate:  open "BASE/noexist/../target.txt" (noexist does not)
The missing case is the UP-vs-BACK discriminator: POSIX-UP physically
walks the directories, so the missing segment can't be traversed and the
open FAILS; a syntactic BACK rewrite just deletes "noexist/../" textually,
so the open SUCCEEDS.

  engine / OS        | ".." via existing dir | ".." via MISSING dir | semantics
  -------------------+-----------------------+----------------------+-----------
  clautolisp (all)   | HANDLE  (resolves)    | NIL    (fails)       | POSIX-UP
  BricsCAD  macOS    | HANDLE  (resolves)    | NIL    (fails)       | POSIX-UP
  BricsCAD  Windows  | HANDLE  (resolves)    | HANDLE (succeeds)    | syntactic-BACK
  AutoCAD   Windows  | HANDLE  (resolves)    | HANDLE (succeeds)    | syntactic-BACK

findfile mirrors open in every cell (collapses ".." when open succeeds,
nil when it fails).

Findings
--------

1. EXISTING-intermediate ".." (and ".") resolve everywhere — clautolisp,
   BricsCAD (both OSes), AutoCAD. There is NO divergence for the common
   case. The issue's original premise ("BricsCAD does NOT resolve '..'")
   is REFUTED: BricsCAD resolves it exactly like clautolisp.

2. The real, narrow divergence is MISSING-intermediate "..", and it is
   OS-level, not engine-level:
     - POSIX (clautolisp everywhere, BricsCAD macOS): UP — the missing
       directory can't be walked, so it FAILS.
     - Windows (BricsCAD Windows, AutoCAD Windows): BACK — the CAD uses
       the Windows path canonicaliser (GetFullPathName-style), which
       collapses ".." TEXTUALLY, so it SUCCEEDS even through a missing
       directory.

3. clautolisp does UP on ALL platforms (it goes through the CL
   truename/open path). So on WINDOWS clautolisp is STRICTER than the
   native Windows CADs: a "BASE/missing/../target.txt" that AutoCAD /
   BricsCAD-Windows open, clautolisp-on-Windows rejects. That is the
   opposite direction from what this ticket assumed.

Reconciling the SCHMS report
----------------------------

The SCHMS failure was on BricsCAD V26 / macOS — i.e. UP semantics. A
"repo-dir/../outils-autolisp/..." path fails under UP whenever the ".."
step lands on a directory the process can't physically walk (a component
that doesn't exist from the CAD's cwd, or a symlink resolving elsewhere),
while a clean absolute path with no ".." always works — exactly what was
observed. clautolisp happened to resolve the same string because its cwd
/ physical layout let the UP walk succeed. So this was never "BricsCAD
can't do ..": it was UP-semantics sensitivity to the physical tree, which
clean paths sidestep.

Bonus: TEMPPREFIX on the real CADs
----------------------------------

The probe also captured real TEMPPREFIX values, which validated the
clautolisp-tempprefix-empty fix (clautolisp 1.7.4):
  BricsCAD macOS   : /var/folders/.../T/BricsCAD/
  BricsCAD Windows : C:\Users\<user>\AppData\Local\Temp\BricsCAD\
  AutoCAD  Windows : C:\Users\PPBN02~1\AppData\Local\Temp\   (8.3 short name)
All non-empty, trailing separator; AutoCAD returns a backslash 8.3 path.

Recommendation (needs a pjb decision)
-------------------------------------

The ticket's two original directives were premised on BricsCAD being the
STRICTER one; the data shows the reverse on Windows and parity on macOS,
so they need revisiting:

  a) "Document that BricsCAD produces different results." -> Amend: on
     macOS BricsCAD == clautolisp (UP); on Windows both CADs are BACK and
     clautolisp is the strict one. Document the OS-level UP/BACK split in
     the source-encoding / pathname section, with this matrix.

  b) "Warn on any /../ in non-clautolisp/lax dialects." -> A BLANKET
     warning is unjustified: existing-intermediate ".." is portable
     everywhere. The only non-portable construct is ".." THROUGH A
     MISSING directory (Windows CADs accept it, clautolisp + macOS
     BricsCAD reject it). Options for pjb:
       (i)  warn only when a ".." segment's preceding directory does not
            exist (requires a probe-file at flag time), or
       (ii) leave clautolisp UP everywhere and just DOCUMENT the gap
            (portable code must not build ".." through possibly-missing
            dirs), or
       (iii) make clautolisp-on-Windows follow the platform (BACK) so it
            matches the native Windows CADs — at the cost of cross-OS
            clautolisp consistency.
     Deferring the choice to pjb; no warning implemented yet.

STATUS: investigation complete; engine behaviour characterised on all
three targets. Left OPEN pending the (a)/(b) documentation + policy
decision. Probe tooling kept (manual CI jobs) so any target can be
re-checked on demand.

===========================================================================
DECISION (pjb, 2026-07-30) — warn on every `..' path; keep UP everywhere.
===========================================================================

Even when both UP and BACK resolve to a file, they differ FUNDAMENTALLY
once a SYMBOLIC LINK precedes the `..': UP walks the physical tree (the
`..' after a symlink lands in the link target's parent), while BACK
rewrites textually (the symlink is ignored). So a `..' path is
non-portable in general, not only in the missing-directory case.

Ruling, implemented in clautolisp 1.7.5:
  - open / findfile / load emit a `[path-dotdot]' portability warning for
    ANY path containing a `..' component, in every dialect EXCEPT --lax
    (--strict, --autocad, --bricscad and --clautolisp all warn; --lax is
    the silent catch-all). See emit-dotdot-path-portability-warning and
    tests/... path-dotdot-tests.lisp.
  - clautolisp keeps POSIX-UP semantics on every platform.

Deferred alternative (noted, not done): clautolisp could instead follow
the platform — BACK on Windows, UP on Unix — as BricsCAD does, so it
would match whichever native CAD the user runs beside. Kept UP everywhere
for now for cross-OS clautolisp consistency; revisit if the divergence
with the Windows CADs proves a problem in practice.

This closes the ticket's two open directives (document the behaviour;
warn on `..' outside --lax). STATUS: the warning has shipped; this issue
stays OPEN only as the record of the platform-dependent alternative
above, to be closed or actioned at pjb's discretion.

===========================================================================
CLOSED (2026-08-06) — both directives shipped; deferred alternative rehomed.
===========================================================================

Closing. The investigation is complete and both original directives are
implemented and verified on master:

  - Behaviour characterised on all three real targets (probe matrix of
    2026-07-30, above): existing-intermediate ".." resolves everywhere;
    the only divergence is missing-intermediate ".." and it is OS-level
    (POSIX-UP: clautolisp everywhere + BricsCAD-macOS; syntactic-BACK:
    BricsCAD-Windows + AutoCAD-Windows). Documented with the matrix.
  - pjb decision (2026-07-30) shipped in clautolisp 1.7.5: open / findfile
    / load emit a `[path-dotdot]' portability warning for ANY ".."
    component in every dialect except --lax, and clautolisp keeps POSIX-UP
    on every platform. Verified present on master:
    emit-dotdot-path-portability-warning (autolisp-runtime + builtins-core)
    and clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/tests/path-dotdot-tests.lisp.

The BLOCKED · bricscad-access label was stale: the probe had already run
on the granted BricsCAD runners (both OSes); the true remaining item was
the pjb close-out, now done.

Deferred alternative (NOT lost): "clautolisp follows the platform — BACK
on Windows, UP on Unix — to match whichever native CAD runs beside it."
This is a platform-dependent path-semantics choice and belongs with the
platform axis, so it is rehomed to dialect-platform-version-axis.issue
(the same axis already governs whether "/..." warns on autocad-windows vs
autocad-mac). Revisit there if the Windows divergence proves a problem in
practice; not scheduled now.
